Date: Wed, 21 Apr 93 9:44:21 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: TECH: Report - Entropic Cost of Computation

Mike (and others),

Thanks for posting your derivation; I'm working
through it with a thermo textbook, and have a
few questions.  I post them to the list because
there are probably a few people who can answer,
if anybody has the inclination.  This material
is all very interesting and I may be nearing completion
(but there are still a few sticking points, only two
of which I mention here)... specific questions:

1) You start with the Gibbs (Shannon) definition
of entropy for a macrostate, S = -k sum p ln p.  I understand
the derivation of this, except for the introduction of
boltzmann's constant.  Obviously, any constant would do;
what I can't understand is the introduction of the units
involved (J/degK).  After deriving a formula that expresses
the randomness of a macrostate, it seems fishy to just tack
on whatever units are convenient.

2) In your derivation, you reduce that definition to the Boltzmann
entropy definition S = k ln W, where W is the number of microstates
in the macrostate.  This is particularly exciting to me for my
phase space view because the volume of phase space corresponding
to a macrostate is exactly W; that result seems like it could
lead directly to entropic costs by considering changes in phase
space volume for non-reversible logic gates.  However, it assumes
that the probabilities of all the quantum states are equal, which
doesn't seem justified.  More generally, the Boltzmann definition
only applies to systems in equilibrium; an operating nanocomputer
is never in equilibrium.  It may be that more complex math would
produce the same result, but I'd rather not bother if I don't have
to.

If you have time for a few hints, thanks!

derek

