From: tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (Dave Burns)
Subject: LAW: Employment contracts

>Tom
>>Dave

>>Think of an extremely
>>tyrannical society. [. . .] couldn't some restriction upon the possible
>>employment agreements increase freedom? 
>
>This example is not useful.  It does not prove that some restrictions on
>employment agreements increase freedom; it only proves that *less
>burdensome* restrictions may do so.  But that is not the issue.  The issue
>is whether a legal system that imposes non-negotiable restrictions on the
>substance (rather than the form) of employment contracts is less free than
>a legal system that does not.
>
>>Dave Burns
>
>T.O. Morrow      

I don't understand. The first half of Tom's paragraph seems to say that
employment restrictions increase freedom if they have the effect of making
total restrictions less burdensome (as in my Soviet Union example). In
effect, these restrictions make the society more free compared to the case
without them. The second half seems to say that restrictions always reduce
freedom.

Restrictions and freedoms are two sides of a door. My freedom to do
something implies restrictions on others. Freedom of the press restricts
the power of government. Restriction on encryption lowers barriers to (both
legitimate and abusive) government action. This employs a broad meaning for
the word 'freedom' (consistent with Tom's criterion from an earlier post,
where he called the society 'which allows a wider range of behaviors' more
free).

To paraphrase Tom's argument:
In the 'right to stink' society, freedom of contract is restricted, while
freedom to stink is unrestricted.

In the 'right to contract' society both are unrestricted, as stinkers are
free to look for jobs where smell is not an issue (stink is still
unrestricted).

Therefore the 'right to stink' society is less free. It allows a narrower
range of behaviors. (end of paraphrase)

How about this?
In the 'right to contract' society, freedom of murder is restricted, while
freedom to contract is unrestricted.

In the 'right to murder' society both are unrestricted.

Therefore the 'right to contract' society is less free. It allows a
narrower range of behaviors. (end of paraphrase)

Contracting is just a means to an end (having no stinking employees). In
both societies it is possible (though not equally easy) for an employer to
achieve this end. So both societies allow identical ends but different
means. How is their restriction of contracts different from a similar
restriction on petty crime, fraud, extortion, etc. (other than
practicality)? Is a society that allows employers to threaten employees
more free yet? By the criterion of range of allowed behavior, a society
with no laws would be most free (an absurdity). Range of behavior is a poor
criterion of freedom. 

