From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: Cult-prone personalities (was Re: Underclass Crime)

On Wed, 21 Apr 1993, Richard Kennaway wrote:

> Here's another interesting correlation.  According to someone I heard
> interviewed on the radio yesterday (a former member of the Waco sect who
> got out before the recent fracas), the people most susceptible to being
> converted by kooky sects are non-religious, independent-minded people of
> above-average intelligence.  Who do we know who fits that description? :-)

This is an interesting correlation, but probably needs further analysis.
I'll leave aside the "above-average intelligence" part until I see some
statistics (I have a feeling that all individuals who consider themselves
"independent-minded" also consider themselves to be above average in
intelligence, but there may be room for doubt as to the reliability of
their self-perceptions).  And "independent-minded" is a pretty fuzzy notion,
which I'd like to see an operationalizable definition for before taking
this too far.  But taking it at its fuzzy face value, it would seem
to have a high correlation with "non-religious".  Perhaps not all
"independent-minded" people are non-religious, but in the U.S., at
least, non-religiosity is probably a pretty good predictor of some
degree of independent-mindedness, since most non-religious people
(at least those not of the current younger generation) must have
strayed away from a religiosity that their parents and other elders
tried to inculcate in them.

So all this putative correlation is really saying is that people not
currently committed to a religion are more likely than others to pick 
up a new one. How surprising is that?  There was talk here a few
weeks ago speculating about a religious meme-site, a hypothesis which
would predict exactly this pattern--if the site is occupied, there's
no room for a new religious meme-complex; if it's not, there's a space
there waiting.

So, to respond to the implication in Richard's final question:  Not
to worry, Extropians!  If you TRULY BELIEVE, you are in no danger.
The doubters and backsliders on the list may find themselves doing
dream work with the Eck gang next year, or holed up in a bunker in 
Arkansas waiting for the Fire next time, but if your religious meme-site 
is properly filled with the Extropian Gospel, all the millenarian siren
songs of the next few years will pass you by.  Secure in our faith, waiting
and planning for the Singularity, we will not be moved.

Scott DeLancey



